Google penalty recovery is the work of finding why a site lost Google traffic and fixing that cause. A traffic drop is not always a penalty. Changing everything at once makes the cause harder to find. A manual action, a core update, a technical fault and a migration each need a different fix. I find the cause first.

## What I check first for each type of Google traffic drop

Each type of Google traffic drop has its own first check and its own route to recovery. I use the table below in the free diagnosis.

| Your situation | What I check first |
|---|---|
| **Manual action** in Search Console | The Manual Actions report, the policy named and the pages affected |
| **Core update drop** | Drop date against Google's confirmed update dates, which pages and queries fell, and what now ranks instead |
| **Technical drop** | Indexing report, noindex and canonical rules, robots.txt, server errors and sitemaps |
| **Migration drop** | Old-to-new redirect map, lost URLs, changed templates and internal links |

The diagnosis tells you which row you are in. Many drops are a mix. One example is a migration that went live in the same month as a core update. I follow [Google's guidance on debugging traffic drops](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/monitor-debug/debugging-search-traffic-drops) for this first pass. It rules out lost demand, tracking changes and stronger competitors before anyone touches the site.

