## What is a Google algorithm update?

A Google algorithm update is a change to the systems Google uses to rank search results. Google makes many small changes each year and several larger core updates. Core updates do not target individual sites. They change how Google assesses content across the web. Some sites rise and others fall. The August 2018 core update is one example. The SEO industry nicknamed it "Medic". Google confirms core updates on its ranking updates page. I match your drop date against it.

## What is a Google penalty?

"Google penalty" is used for two different things: a manual action and an algorithmic drop. The recovery route differs for each.

{% panels %}
{% panel eyebrow="Google penalty, type 1" title="Manual action" %}
- **Who applies it:** a human reviewer at Google after a spam policy breach
- **Where it shows:** Search Console under Security & Manual Actions > Manual Actions. The report names the problem
- **Common causes:** unnatural links, thin affiliate content, cloaking, hidden text and user-generated spam
- **Recovery route:** fix the stated violation and submit a reconsideration request
{% /panel %}
{% panel eyebrow="Google penalty, type 2" title="Algorithmic drop" %}
- **Who applies it:** nobody. Google's ranking systems reassessed your site
- **Where it shows:** nowhere. Search Console says "No issues detected"
- **Possible causes:** weaker content than what now ranks, intent mismatch or gaps in topic coverage. The audit tests each
- **Recovery route:** improve the pages that fell and wait for Google to reassess
{% /panel %}
{% /panels %}

Manual actions follow a breach of [Google's spam policies](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/essentials/spam-policies). Algorithmic drops are not penalties in the strict sense. Google sends no notification for them. That makes them harder to diagnose. "No issues detected" in the Manual Actions report does not prove an algorithm change caused the drop. Technical faults, lost demand, tracking changes and stronger competitors all look the same on a traffic graph.

## How do I recover from a Google algorithm update?

Recovery from a Google algorithm update follows the six steps below. The free diagnosis gives my view on step one. The £495 audit completes steps one to three and sets the order for step four.

{% process %}
{% step title="Confirm the cause" detail="Manual Actions, indexing, tracking and the drop date against Google's update history." /%}
{% step title="Find what fell and why" detail="Which pages and queries lost ground and what pattern they share." /%}
{% step title="Audit the pages that fell" detail="Each page that fell compared with what ranks now." /%}
{% step title="Fix in priority order" detail="Technical faults and stated violations first. Then existing pages before new ones." /%}
{% step title="Request review or wait for reassessment" detail="A reconsideration request for a manual action. Reassessment over time for an algorithmic drop." /%}
{% step title="Monitor and adjust" detail="Rankings and enquiries after each fix. Reassess if the next core update does not help." /%}
{% /process %}

### Confirm the drop is update-related

Confirm the cause before you change anything. Not every drop is caused by an update.

Check [Google's confirmed update history](https://developers.google.com/search/updates/ranking) against the date of your drop. Check in Search Console whether impressions dropped or only clicks. Look for crawl errors, accidental noindex tags or a broken sitemap. These cause drops that look like an update but have nothing to do with Google's ranking systems.

### Identify what fell and why

Export ranking and Search Console data for the weeks around the drop. List the pages and queries that lost ground.

- **Losses mainly on informational queries** often point to content quality, depth or [E-E-A-T](/blog/what-is-eeat-seo/) signals.
- **Losses on commercial queries** can point to intent mismatch: the page no longer matches what Google thinks the searcher wants.
- **Losses in one section while others held** point to a topic-specific problem rather than a site-wide one.

### Audit the content that fell

Ask three questions about each important page that lost rankings. Does it show real expertise and first-hand experience? Does it answer the query as fully as the pages that now outrank it? Is it clear who wrote it and why they can be trusted?

### Audit the pages that fell: check topical coverage

Check topical coverage by mapping every subtopic your buyers search for. Compare your coverage with the pages that now outrank yours.

Map your content against a full [topical map](/services/topical-maps/) of your subject. Find what is covered well, what is thin and what is missing. Close the biggest gaps first.

### Fix in priority order: improve before you scale

Improve existing pages before publishing new ones. Updating the pages that fell tests the likely cause directly. More content of the same quality does not. Update, expand and strengthen the pages that fell first.

### Manual penalty recovery: fix violations and request review

Manual penalty recovery has a fixed route. Audit the backlink profile where links are the problem. Attempt removal of unnatural links and disavow the ones you cannot remove. Improve or consolidate thin and duplicate pages where content is the problem. Then submit a reconsideration request in Search Console. Be specific about what you found, what you fixed and how you will stop it recurring. Vague requests are more likely to be rejected. Google penalty removal is the lifting of the manual action after a successful review.

### Monitor and adjust

Track rankings, impressions and enquiries after each fix. If the next core update does not help, reassess what is left and repeat the audit on the pages still down.

## What triggers recovery for each type of drop

Recovery from a Google traffic drop needs a different trigger for each cause. Google decides the timing for three of the four.

| Type of drop | What has to happen | Who decides |
|---|---|---|
| Technical fault | The fault is fixed and the affected pages are recrawled | Googlebot on recrawl |
| Manual action | The violation is fixed and a reconsideration request is accepted | A Google reviewer |
| Core update drop | The pages that fell are improved and reassessed | Google's ranking systems, often at the next core update |
| Migration drop | Redirects and missing pages are repaired and recrawled | Googlebot on recrawl |

## What does not work for Google penalty recovery

Three reactions often make a Google traffic drop harder to recover from.

- **Mass content deletion.** It sounds logical but often removes pages that were supporting your topic coverage.
- **Aggressive link building straight after a drop.** Google is assessing quality. A higher link count does not answer that.
- **Reverting to a backup from before the update.** Restoring old content does not address the cause and can undo fixes made since.

Avoid these and the other [common SEO mistakes](/blog/seo-mistakes/) that make a drop worse. Google's guidance on core updates points to helpful, people-first content as the thing to improve. Work on it only after technical faults, lost demand and tracking changes have been ruled out.

{% cta heading="Get the cause confirmed before you change anything" text="The free 20-minute diagnosis tells you which type of drop you are dealing with and whether the £495 audit is the right next step." /%}

## Frequently asked questions

### How long does Google penalty recovery take?

Google penalty recovery takes anywhere from days to several months depending on the cause. A technical fault can recover soon after it is fixed and recrawled. A manual action recovers after Google reviews your reconsideration request.

Core update drops take longer. [Google's guidance on core updates](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/core-updates) says changes can take effect in a few days. It also says its systems could take several months to confirm a site has improved. That could mean waiting until the next core update.

### Can you guarantee recovery after a Google algorithm update?

No. Nobody can guarantee recovery after a Google algorithm update. Google decides how pages are reassessed. What I can do is find the most likely cause, fix it properly and track the result. Some sites need more than one update cycle.

### How do I know if I have a Google penalty or an algorithm drop?

The Manual Actions report in Google Search Console tells you whether you have a Google penalty. Open Security & Manual Actions > Manual Actions. A listed issue means you have a manual action. "No issues detected" means the drop is algorithmic, technical or caused by something else. Lost demand and tracking changes are two common examples.

### Do you offer Google penalty removal?

Yes. Google penalty removal is the manual action part of my recovery service: find the violation, fix it, document it and submit the reconsideration request. Google lifts the manual action after a successful review. An algorithmic drop has no removal process. That work is recovery rather than removal.

### Do you offer a Google penalty recovery service in the UK?

Yes. My Google penalty recovery service is run from Reading in Berkshire and works online with sites across the UK. The process is the same wherever you are based. The free diagnosis comes first. The £495 audit or ongoing support follows if you need it.

### Can you recover traffic lost after a site migration?

Yes when the cause is missing redirects, lost pages or changed templates. Those are the common causes of a migration drop and each can be repaired.

My [SEO migration consultant](/services/seo-migration-consultant/) service covers redirects, lost URLs and templates.

### Should I disavow links after a drop?

Usually no. Disavow links after a drop only when there is a link-related manual action or clear evidence of a link scheme. Disavowing low-quality links that carry no risk does not fix a core update drop.

